WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.186 --> 00:00:02.523
[audience applauds]
[audience cheers]

00:00:02.523 --> 00:00:04.380
[gunshot blasts]

00:00:04.380 --> 00:00:05.760
Ken Bombace: That's part of our job.

00:00:05.760 --> 00:00:08.360
What I don't like is that he
pulls his weapon immediately.

00:00:08.360 --> 00:00:11.390
He would probably be the
person tackled if he did that.

00:00:11.390 --> 00:00:12.900
Hi, my name is Ken Bombace,

00:00:12.900 --> 00:00:15.767
and I have over 20 years of
law-enforcement experience.

00:00:15.767 --> 00:00:19.108
I've led a company as the CEO
of Global Threat Solutions,

00:00:19.108 --> 00:00:21.490
which is a protection firm that provides

00:00:21.490 --> 00:00:25.070
comprehensive protection
services to executives,

00:00:25.070 --> 00:00:27.410
people of high net worth, and celebrities.

00:00:27.410 --> 00:00:30.160
And today I'm gonna be
reviewing movie and TV clips

00:00:30.160 --> 00:00:33.260
of bodyguard scenes and
rating them for realism.

00:00:37.472 --> 00:00:39.389
Creasy: Pita, you have a pencil?

00:00:43.540 --> 00:00:45.610
Ken: You're never going to be distracted.

00:00:45.610 --> 00:00:47.530
You don't text. You don't make calls.

00:00:47.530 --> 00:00:49.300
You're definitely not gonna
be writing something down

00:00:49.300 --> 00:00:50.970
while you're driving a client.

00:00:50.970 --> 00:00:53.430
Protection agents will
have what we call a go bag,

00:00:53.430 --> 00:00:55.170
a tactical bag, a go bag,

00:00:55.170 --> 00:00:57.030
and you're gonna have
everything you need in there.

00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.660
You'll even put some
food, drinks in there.

00:00:59.660 --> 00:01:02.220
You might have to sit in
that vehicle for hours.

00:01:02.220 --> 00:01:04.140
To do your job, you'd have a flashlight.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:05.990
You have your laptop with you.

00:01:05.990 --> 00:01:08.270
You're gonna have your phone, chargers,

00:01:08.270 --> 00:01:10.080
backup battery chargers.

00:01:10.080 --> 00:01:12.550
Something to write with, pens
if you have to take notes

00:01:12.550 --> 00:01:14.500
when your principal's
giving you some instructions

00:01:14.500 --> 00:01:16.210
or maybe a staff member.

00:01:16.210 --> 00:01:17.660
These are just some of the things that

00:01:17.660 --> 00:01:20.082
you might have with you at any time.

00:01:20.082 --> 00:01:22.415
[dog barks]

00:01:24.650 --> 00:01:27.425
And might a protection agent be tasked

00:01:27.425 --> 00:01:29.500
with watching the principal's dog

00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:30.900
while they're in a meeting or something?

00:01:30.900 --> 00:01:32.867
Sure, it does happen.

00:01:32.867 --> 00:01:34.894
Creasy: Run! [gunshot blasts]

00:01:36.760 --> 00:01:39.140
Ken: You definitely
don't fire warning shots

00:01:39.140 --> 00:01:41.100
in a situation like this.

00:01:41.100 --> 00:01:42.700
I understand it's part of the storyline,

00:01:42.700 --> 00:01:44.490
but that's something we don't do.

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:47.330
Your mission, and this
applies to every clip

00:01:47.330 --> 00:01:49.890
that I'll talk about, in
any protection operation

00:01:49.890 --> 00:01:52.910
is to get the principal
off "the X," we call it.

00:01:52.910 --> 00:01:55.910
The X being the location of danger,

00:01:55.910 --> 00:01:58.140
the immediate vicinity of danger.

00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.638
Our job is to get them out of there

00:01:59.638 --> 00:02:01.463
as fast as we can, and that's it.

00:02:01.463 --> 00:02:04.780
[gunshots blast]

00:02:04.780 --> 00:02:06.538
Firefights and shoot-outs,

00:02:06.538 --> 00:02:08.180
whether in the military or in police,

00:02:08.180 --> 00:02:10.010
are chaotic and confusing.

00:02:10.010 --> 00:02:11.167
When I was in the military,

00:02:11.167 --> 00:02:13.880
obviously I was involved in firefights

00:02:13.880 --> 00:02:16.600
when I was in Iraq as an army officer.

00:02:16.600 --> 00:02:18.360
I think they did a pretty good job

00:02:18.360 --> 00:02:20.587
at depicting a shoot-out like that.

00:02:20.587 --> 00:02:24.070
For the circumstances he
had, with a one-man detail,

00:02:24.070 --> 00:02:26.303
he did the best he
could in that situation.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:31.120
Creasy: Pita! Run! [gunshot blasts]

00:02:31.120 --> 00:02:33.450
Ken: Some training goes
in on their side too,

00:02:33.450 --> 00:02:34.750
meaning the principal.

00:02:34.750 --> 00:02:36.703
So, code words, yes, that happens.

00:02:36.703 --> 00:02:38.767
It can be, maybe you see something

00:02:38.767 --> 00:02:41.590
that's suspicious that they don't know.

00:02:41.590 --> 00:02:43.399
So you would use a word
rather than just say,

00:02:43.399 --> 00:02:45.160
"Hey, watch out. There's a threat."

00:02:45.160 --> 00:02:47.129
I would give it a seven.

00:02:47.129 --> 00:02:50.551
[crowd cheering]

00:02:50.551 --> 00:02:51.962
Bodyguard: Ma'am, excuse
me, if you don't mind,

00:02:51.962 --> 00:02:53.734
I suggest we keep moving.

00:02:54.592 --> 00:02:55.970
Bodyguard: Ma'am, let's go. Let's go.

00:02:55.970 --> 00:02:59.560
Ken: I've done a celebrity
detail for a big award show,

00:02:59.560 --> 00:03:01.890
and when my principal exited the vehicle,

00:03:01.890 --> 00:03:06.730
he decided to run about a
half a mile down a street

00:03:06.730 --> 00:03:09.330
crowded with fans and
slapping all of their hands,

00:03:09.330 --> 00:03:11.032
and I had to run right alongside of him.

00:03:11.032 --> 00:03:13.653
There was no telling him not to.

00:03:13.653 --> 00:03:17.640
♪ La, la, la, la, la, la, la, la, la. ♪

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.020
Ken: They're not gonna be in a room where

00:03:19.020 --> 00:03:21.900
the celebrity is actually getting changed,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:23.900
but rehearsing, prepping in a green room,

00:03:23.900 --> 00:03:26.360
absolutely, it's very
likely you would have

00:03:26.360 --> 00:03:28.290
a protection agent in there.

00:03:28.290 --> 00:03:29.123
Bryan: Excuse me, miss.

00:03:29.123 --> 00:03:30.250
I know this isn't the right time,

00:03:30.250 --> 00:03:31.970
but I have a daughter
who wants to be a singer,

00:03:31.970 --> 00:03:34.210
and I was wondering if
you had any tips for her.

00:03:34.210 --> 00:03:37.860
Ken: As the CEO of a protection firm,

00:03:37.860 --> 00:03:40.460
that is what I refer to
as a fireable offense.

00:03:40.460 --> 00:03:41.326
You do not,

00:03:41.326 --> 00:03:45.142
as your celebrity client
is about to head onstage,

00:03:45.142 --> 00:03:47.748
stop her in front of a
group of all of her staff

00:03:47.748 --> 00:03:49.440
and ask her for a personal favor

00:03:49.440 --> 00:03:51.260
or a personal opinion.

00:03:51.260 --> 00:03:53.040
That is something we do not do.

00:03:53.040 --> 00:03:54.465
Totally unprofessional.

00:03:54.465 --> 00:03:55.600
Bodyguard: Waiting on an airstrike.

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:57.300
And we're waiting, and we're waiting.

00:03:57.300 --> 00:03:59.415
[Bryan's phone rings]

00:03:59.415 --> 00:04:00.248
Bryan: Hello.

00:04:00.248 --> 00:04:02.920
Ken: They may not be assigned
to go out into the crowd,

00:04:02.920 --> 00:04:04.353
but that does not mean
you're playing cards

00:04:04.353 --> 00:04:05.711
in the back room.

00:04:05.711 --> 00:04:08.190
You need to be right backstage
monitoring everything,

00:04:08.190 --> 00:04:09.648
not playing cards.

00:04:09.648 --> 00:04:12.398
[dramatic music]

00:04:13.350 --> 00:04:16.180
That part right there, I would
say, is pretty realistic.

00:04:16.180 --> 00:04:19.630
She exits the stage,
there's chaos obviously,

00:04:19.630 --> 00:04:22.610
this is a large venue, big concert.

00:04:22.610 --> 00:04:24.740
There's venue security and venue staff

00:04:24.740 --> 00:04:26.280
that you have no control over there.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:27.780
So, sure, that can happen.

00:04:27.780 --> 00:04:29.038
What do they do? They move her quickly

00:04:29.038 --> 00:04:30.618
and professionally out of there.

00:04:30.618 --> 00:04:32.827
Bryan: It's OK. It's OK. You're safe now.

00:04:35.957 --> 00:04:37.660
Ken: You know, they're
human, and you're human.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:40.380
As long as you're doing your job,

00:04:40.380 --> 00:04:42.808
you could obviously support them

00:04:42.808 --> 00:04:45.140
to make them feel better
during this tough time.

00:04:45.140 --> 00:04:47.281
I've had scenarios where principals

00:04:47.281 --> 00:04:49.497
are emotional and upset over something

00:04:49.497 --> 00:04:52.330
that happens, or afraid, quite frankly.

00:04:52.330 --> 00:04:54.860
And they do show their appreciation.

00:04:54.860 --> 00:04:57.360
You would just wanna guard against

00:04:57.360 --> 00:04:59.110
keeping boundaries up.

00:04:59.110 --> 00:05:02.184
You don't want to get too
close in this industry,

00:05:02.184 --> 00:05:04.650
so you maintain a certain
level of professionalism.

00:05:04.650 --> 00:05:07.259
I would expect him after that interaction

00:05:07.259 --> 00:05:10.090
to let me know about it, that it happened.

00:05:10.090 --> 00:05:12.333
I'll give it a six.

00:05:14.190 --> 00:05:15.883
[Cookie screaming]

00:05:15.883 --> 00:05:17.966
[punching]

00:05:17.966 --> 00:05:19.290
Cookie: Yeah, yeah!

00:05:19.290 --> 00:05:21.803
I think the fight scene
was pretty unrealistic.

00:05:21.803 --> 00:05:24.103
They're having a fistfight in the street,

00:05:24.103 --> 00:05:26.030
but then another subject
who was the driver

00:05:26.030 --> 00:05:27.375
or someone else is assisting

00:05:27.375 --> 00:05:29.420
and kicking the person on the ground.

00:05:29.420 --> 00:05:30.961
Could there be untrained people

00:05:30.961 --> 00:05:32.344
that call themselves bodyguards

00:05:32.344 --> 00:05:34.497
that do that sort of thing? Sure.

00:05:34.497 --> 00:05:36.602
Malcolm: You know, this
is a bad neighborhood.

00:05:36.602 --> 00:05:40.050
Cookie: Aw, you worried
about me? I'm fine.

00:05:40.050 --> 00:05:42.580
Ken: It does happen, where
people can be inebriated.

00:05:42.580 --> 00:05:43.780
They can be belligerent.

00:05:43.780 --> 00:05:45.600
You have to remember this person is,

00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:48.210
all intents and purposes,
your boss, the principal.

00:05:48.210 --> 00:05:50.568
So you may rely on staff members

00:05:50.568 --> 00:05:51.660
that you work closely with,

00:05:51.660 --> 00:05:54.150
non-security people who
work closely with them,

00:05:54.150 --> 00:05:56.250
or even family members to intervene.

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:57.660
You also wanna protect them against

00:05:57.660 --> 00:05:58.990
things like embarrassment.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:01.500
There's a famous scene
where somebody popped out

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.850
and hit Bill Gates in the face with a pie.

00:06:03.850 --> 00:06:07.410
That is a tragic fail
for a protection team

00:06:07.410 --> 00:06:08.950
for that to have been able to happen.

00:06:08.950 --> 00:06:10.530
Was he assassinated? No.

00:06:10.530 --> 00:06:11.496
But was he embarrassed?

00:06:11.496 --> 00:06:14.545
Was he not happy with that scenario?

00:06:14.545 --> 00:06:17.805
Is that a liability to him
as a businessman? Sure.

00:06:17.805 --> 00:06:19.930
Cookie: Lucious, Lucious, Lucious.

00:06:19.930 --> 00:06:22.330
Malcolm: Are you OK with
the way he looks at you?

00:06:22.330 --> 00:06:24.170
Whatever that is, that's too intense.

00:06:24.170 --> 00:06:25.560
Ken: Of course, it could happen.

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:27.155
Yeah, you can't leave them

00:06:27.155 --> 00:06:29.496
during a situation where
they could get hurt

00:06:29.496 --> 00:06:31.404
or they're vulnerable, of course.

00:06:31.404 --> 00:06:33.250
So, yes, might you physically have to?

00:06:33.250 --> 00:06:34.741
You would, if that's the scenario.

00:06:34.741 --> 00:06:36.260
And people, you know,

00:06:36.260 --> 00:06:38.493
principals are people too.

00:06:38.493 --> 00:06:40.460
Malcolm: I've done a thorough review

00:06:40.460 --> 00:06:43.568
of both your home and your
corporate headquarters.

00:06:43.568 --> 00:06:45.010
Lucious: Really?

00:06:45.010 --> 00:06:47.318
Ken: Yes, a comprehensive assessment

00:06:47.318 --> 00:06:50.130
on an estate or residence

00:06:50.130 --> 00:06:51.755
and the corporate facility,

00:06:51.755 --> 00:06:53.050
of course we would do that.

00:06:53.050 --> 00:06:54.008
But what I would say is

00:06:54.008 --> 00:06:55.708
you would not do it most likely

00:06:55.708 --> 00:06:58.860
without the principals knowing
about it and assisting,

00:06:58.860 --> 00:07:00.135
because you wouldn't have access

00:07:00.135 --> 00:07:01.593
to everything that you'd need

00:07:01.593 --> 00:07:04.230
to really do a comprehensive
security assessment.

00:07:04.230 --> 00:07:06.070
That part of it, where
he sort of surprised him

00:07:06.070 --> 00:07:08.080
with it, saying, "I've already done it,"

00:07:08.080 --> 00:07:10.073
there's only so far you can go.

00:07:14.690 --> 00:07:17.040
Might you in a certain scenario

00:07:17.040 --> 00:07:18.425
use your body to tackle

00:07:18.425 --> 00:07:21.975
or at least shield your principal?

00:07:21.975 --> 00:07:23.225
Sure, that can happen

00:07:23.225 --> 00:07:25.340
if there's a great enough threat to them.

00:07:25.340 --> 00:07:27.830
Would you do it in this
scenario? Probably not.

00:07:27.830 --> 00:07:30.020
It's an unrealistic
scenario to begin with,

00:07:30.020 --> 00:07:31.373
but you would probably not do it.

00:07:31.373 --> 00:07:33.250
It might be the last
time you worked for them.

00:07:33.250 --> 00:07:35.370
It's a thinking person's game.

00:07:35.370 --> 00:07:37.810
This is not a brute-force game.

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:39.419
You need people with an intellect.

00:07:39.419 --> 00:07:41.040
You need people who can
think on their feet,

00:07:41.040 --> 00:07:44.310
maintain calm when things get hectic.

00:07:44.310 --> 00:07:46.710
And that could be not necessarily

00:07:46.710 --> 00:07:48.740
an immediate threat to your principal.

00:07:48.740 --> 00:07:52.030
You have developed an entire
itinerary for the day.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:53.550
You've done advanced site visits.

00:07:53.550 --> 00:07:55.707
And in a moment's notice they
say, "We changed our mind.

00:07:55.707 --> 00:07:57.730
Now we're going here, to another state."

00:07:57.730 --> 00:07:59.690
Those are the things, you
have to be able to adapt

00:07:59.690 --> 00:08:03.100
and continue seamlessly with your mission.

00:08:03.100 --> 00:08:04.500
It takes a certain type of person

00:08:04.500 --> 00:08:05.890
to be able to do this work.

00:08:05.890 --> 00:08:08.380
You cannot panic easily.

00:08:08.380 --> 00:08:11.000
You need to be able to
maintain calm at all times.

00:08:11.000 --> 00:08:13.210
Do we have some people in this industry

00:08:13.210 --> 00:08:16.770
who are 275 pounds and 6-foot-4? Sure.

00:08:16.770 --> 00:08:19.120
Is that the norm? Absolutely not.

00:08:19.120 --> 00:08:21.260
You do want someone who's physically fit,

00:08:21.260 --> 00:08:24.170
who has a good appearance
that they can do this job.

00:08:24.170 --> 00:08:26.910
Aesthetics are important,
especially for many principals,

00:08:26.910 --> 00:08:29.230
but we do not need somebody
who's a bodybuilder,

00:08:29.230 --> 00:08:31.690
who's a mixed-martial-arts
fighter, necessarily,

00:08:31.690 --> 00:08:32.710
to do this job.

00:08:32.710 --> 00:08:34.360
That's a one. I don't like zeros.

00:08:41.810 --> 00:08:44.590
You would want to know as
much about them as possible.

00:08:44.590 --> 00:08:46.840
She even reviewed her social media

00:08:46.840 --> 00:08:48.156
for anything she might need to know

00:08:48.156 --> 00:08:50.430
about her lifestyle or any concerns,

00:08:50.430 --> 00:08:52.580
but also it goes so much deeper than that.

00:08:52.580 --> 00:08:55.100
We have an onboarding process, really,

00:08:55.100 --> 00:08:57.750
where you're gonna wanna
know their blood type,

00:08:57.750 --> 00:08:59.100
you're gonna wanna know if they have

00:08:59.100 --> 00:09:01.270
any medical conditions or allergies.

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:02.578
In the modern era,

00:09:02.578 --> 00:09:04.803
you can find out a lot about people

00:09:04.803 --> 00:09:06.910
just from open-source intelligence,

00:09:06.910 --> 00:09:08.576
just by using the internet

00:09:08.576 --> 00:09:10.326
and doing open-source searches.

00:09:13.098 --> 00:09:14.923
Zoe: Who do you think you are? My mom?

00:09:15.800 --> 00:09:17.266
Ken: Would you stand around

00:09:17.266 --> 00:09:18.970
looking like a security agent

00:09:18.970 --> 00:09:20.156
in the corner of the bar

00:09:20.156 --> 00:09:22.148
watching them or in close proximity?

00:09:22.148 --> 00:09:23.020
No.

00:09:23.020 --> 00:09:24.317
A lot of times what you would do

00:09:24.317 --> 00:09:27.420
is go sit at the bar or
go sit at your own table,

00:09:27.420 --> 00:09:28.900
act like you're eating, have a drink,

00:09:28.900 --> 00:09:30.782
maybe a nonalcoholic drink,

00:09:30.782 --> 00:09:33.710
and keep an eye on them, while looking,

00:09:33.710 --> 00:09:36.370
it's something we would
call covert protection.

00:09:36.370 --> 00:09:37.720
So you don't wanna be overt.

00:09:37.720 --> 00:09:40.780
Pretty realistic in every aspect.

00:09:40.780 --> 00:09:42.038
I'm gonna give it an eight.

00:09:47.800 --> 00:09:49.742
Executive protection agents don't have

00:09:49.742 --> 00:09:53.202
hidden exotic-weapon vaults

00:09:53.202 --> 00:09:55.980
that they use to choose
their weapons for the day.

00:09:55.980 --> 00:09:59.500
They definitely don't choose
dual silenced pistols.

00:09:59.500 --> 00:10:02.260
And they also don't
grab them by the trigger

00:10:02.260 --> 00:10:03.790
when they pull them out of that vault,

00:10:03.790 --> 00:10:06.620
which is something we learn
day one in the military

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.347
or in the police service,

00:10:08.347 --> 00:10:10.340
is that, they call it "TF out of TG."

00:10:10.340 --> 00:10:12.560
Keep your trigger finger
out of your trigger guard.

00:10:12.560 --> 00:10:14.743
And he violates every one of those rules.

00:10:19.379 --> 00:10:21.904
Sometimes we travel in motorcades,

00:10:21.904 --> 00:10:23.240
more than one vehicle, of course,

00:10:23.240 --> 00:10:25.720
lead and follow vehicles if
the threat's high enough,

00:10:25.720 --> 00:10:27.510
depending on the size of your detail.

00:10:27.510 --> 00:10:29.508
They depart and arrive together, however.

00:10:29.508 --> 00:10:31.560
They don't join the principal's vehicle

00:10:31.560 --> 00:10:35.186
at high speeds and in
very close proximity.

00:10:35.186 --> 00:10:37.670
Michael: Get the engines started.
I want an immediate takeoff.

00:10:37.670 --> 00:10:39.604
Airspace has already been cleared.

00:10:39.604 --> 00:10:40.517
Pilot: Roger that.

00:10:40.517 --> 00:10:42.425
Ken: We definitely do
not have the authority

00:10:42.425 --> 00:10:43.841
to clear airspace

00:10:43.841 --> 00:10:45.530
in the private-protection industry.

00:10:45.530 --> 00:10:46.638
I've never heard of that happening.

00:10:46.638 --> 00:10:49.290
Sometimes clients will
wait a considerable time

00:10:49.290 --> 00:10:50.970
on the tarmac before departure.

00:10:50.970 --> 00:10:53.560
We do a lot of work in Los
Angeles and in New York.

00:10:53.560 --> 00:10:55.429
They will use what we call FBOs.

00:10:55.429 --> 00:10:57.140
Those are the private air terminals.

00:10:57.140 --> 00:10:59.000
They will use those at major airports,

00:10:59.000 --> 00:11:02.030
but oftentimes their FBOs that that we use

00:11:02.030 --> 00:11:05.321
are in smaller regional airports

00:11:05.321 --> 00:11:07.895
close to major airports.

00:11:10.240 --> 00:11:12.850
You probably would not
roll out the blue carpet

00:11:12.850 --> 00:11:15.410
and have a ceremony on the tarmac

00:11:15.410 --> 00:11:16.840
as you're getting them into the aircraft.

00:11:16.840 --> 00:11:19.551
It would probably be a covert departure.

00:11:19.551 --> 00:11:22.195
Bodyguard: Nothing for
miles, chief. Almost boring.

00:11:22.195 --> 00:11:24.190
Michael: And what's our motto?

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:26.050
Bodyguards: Boring is always best.

00:11:26.050 --> 00:11:27.540
Ken: You would definitely not line up

00:11:27.540 --> 00:11:30.070
all of your detailed
vehicles in this manner,

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:31.240
having everybody stand there.

00:11:31.240 --> 00:11:32.920
This is something I've never seen.

00:11:32.920 --> 00:11:34.330
I can guarantee you they wouldn't have

00:11:34.330 --> 00:11:36.040
their private-security helicopter

00:11:36.040 --> 00:11:38.310
hovering over the runway too.

00:11:38.310 --> 00:11:39.690
That would not be allowed.

00:11:41.119 --> 00:11:41.985
[woman screaming]

00:11:41.985 --> 00:11:43.527
Bodyguard: Bravo one down.

00:11:43.527 --> 00:11:45.193
We need a cleanup team in here now!

00:11:45.193 --> 00:11:46.938
Ken: They mentioned, "Get
the cleanup team in here."

00:11:46.938 --> 00:11:48.100
We don't have cleanup teams

00:11:48.100 --> 00:11:50.700
for when principals are assassinated.

00:11:50.700 --> 00:11:53.540
The only cleanup team you're
gonna need is for your career,

00:11:53.540 --> 00:11:54.845
because it's probably gonna be over

00:11:54.845 --> 00:11:56.510
if something like this happens.

00:11:56.510 --> 00:11:58.310
We have people visit every location.

00:11:58.310 --> 00:11:59.660
They're gonna go days in advance,

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:01.441
meet with points of contact,

00:12:01.441 --> 00:12:02.970
plan what route they're gonna take

00:12:02.970 --> 00:12:04.960
in and out of that building.

00:12:04.960 --> 00:12:06.840
We have to plan safe houses.

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:09.650
We have to know the closest
Level I trauma center

00:12:09.650 --> 00:12:11.270
in case there is an incident like this

00:12:11.270 --> 00:12:13.140
where someone gets shot or hurt.

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:14.448
We have to have safe houses,

00:12:14.448 --> 00:12:16.273
which might be a police station close by

00:12:16.273 --> 00:12:17.519
or someplace we know we can bring

00:12:17.519 --> 00:12:18.890
the principal if there's a problem.

00:12:18.890 --> 00:12:21.560
There's contingencies for everything.

00:12:21.560 --> 00:12:22.697
But I can tell you,

00:12:22.697 --> 00:12:25.770
assassinations like this in
principals, extremely rare.

00:12:25.770 --> 00:12:27.042
I would say, I would give it a one

00:12:27.042 --> 00:12:29.250
just because I don't
wanna give anybody a zero.

00:12:29.250 --> 00:12:31.710
Donovan: In a populated
place, a department store,

00:12:31.710 --> 00:12:33.410
I always walk ahead of you.

00:12:33.410 --> 00:12:34.870
I don't like more than
five people between us,

00:12:34.870 --> 00:12:36.720
so if you ditch me
because my back is to you,

00:12:36.720 --> 00:12:37.940
that would be too far.

00:12:37.940 --> 00:12:40.430
Ken: We don't walk in
front of the principal.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.750
You don't do that because
you can't see them.

00:12:42.750 --> 00:12:44.566
If you're a one-man detail,

00:12:44.566 --> 00:12:47.300
like he is in this scenario,

00:12:47.300 --> 00:12:49.520
protocol would say
usually to the rear right,

00:12:49.520 --> 00:12:51.940
just offset, not directly behind them,

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:53.580
but offset to the rear right.

00:12:53.580 --> 00:12:56.090
Now, they approach a door.

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:57.500
Are you gonna just step out in front

00:12:57.500 --> 00:12:58.791
and maybe get that door for them?

00:12:58.791 --> 00:12:59.766
Sure. You can do that.

00:12:59.766 --> 00:13:01.180
You have visual all the time,

00:13:01.180 --> 00:13:03.260
and you're staying in close proximity.

00:13:03.260 --> 00:13:04.554
Might you have a principal that tells you,

00:13:04.554 --> 00:13:05.627
"Don't get the door for me.

00:13:05.627 --> 00:13:07.180
I don't like people getting doors for me"?

00:13:07.180 --> 00:13:09.000
Yeah, I've had that too.

00:13:09.000 --> 00:13:11.110
So it all depends on
the preference of them,

00:13:11.110 --> 00:13:13.770
but you're definitely not
gonna walk in front of them,

00:13:13.770 --> 00:13:14.940
because you know what happens?

00:13:14.940 --> 00:13:16.460
You turn around and they're gone.

00:13:16.460 --> 00:13:17.690
Donovan: At the risk
of being ungentlemanly,

00:13:17.690 --> 00:13:20.910
I can't carry bags. My hands
always have to be free.

00:13:20.910 --> 00:13:23.600
Ken: People do get bags here and there.

00:13:23.600 --> 00:13:26.230
Are we used to exclusively do that,

00:13:26.230 --> 00:13:27.920
and we're loading their packages

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:30.570
and not watching them and
jeopardizing security?

00:13:30.570 --> 00:13:33.097
No, we're not. Do you help your principal

00:13:33.097 --> 00:13:34.780
when you're getting them into a car,

00:13:34.780 --> 00:13:36.992
put a bag in the trunk
and take that for them?

00:13:36.992 --> 00:13:41.720
This is a customer-service-based business.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:43.887
Hogan: What is it that
you look for exactly?

00:13:47.460 --> 00:13:50.240
Ken: Before he decides he's
gonna walk out and chat with her

00:13:50.240 --> 00:13:52.750
and tell her a little
bit about what he does,

00:13:52.750 --> 00:13:55.993
you see he glances over
to the dressing room area.

00:13:55.993 --> 00:13:57.780
As far as I could see in the scene,

00:13:57.780 --> 00:13:58.900
she's the only one there.

00:13:58.900 --> 00:14:00.505
He feels comfortable.

00:14:00.505 --> 00:14:02.468
Donovan: The guy in the coat.

00:14:02.468 --> 00:14:03.930
Hogan: What about him?

00:14:03.930 --> 00:14:05.747
Donovan: Beats me. Why
is he wearing a coat?

00:14:05.747 --> 00:14:06.580
Hogan: I don't know.

00:14:06.580 --> 00:14:07.413
Donovan: I don't know either.

00:14:07.413 --> 00:14:09.890
Ken: There's two kinds of
things I'd be looking for.

00:14:09.890 --> 00:14:11.783
One of them would be someone who has

00:14:11.783 --> 00:14:14.660
an unnatural interest in my principal.

00:14:14.660 --> 00:14:15.810
They're watching them.

00:14:15.810 --> 00:14:17.980
They seem to be in every store they're in.

00:14:17.980 --> 00:14:20.540
That would raise alarms for me.

00:14:20.540 --> 00:14:22.920
Also, there could be a threat by somebody

00:14:22.920 --> 00:14:25.280
just in regular life, anyplace.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:26.570
That threat might not even know

00:14:26.570 --> 00:14:29.300
who your principal is or
that you're even there.

00:14:29.300 --> 00:14:32.950
But sometimes things happen in
public where somebody could,

00:14:32.950 --> 00:14:34.850
maybe there's fights that could break out

00:14:34.850 --> 00:14:36.185
or maybe there's somebody who's

00:14:36.185 --> 00:14:39.080
an emotionally disturbed
person visibly, you can see.

00:14:39.080 --> 00:14:40.960
Those are things you would
want to keep an eye on.

00:14:40.960 --> 00:14:43.265
We have a rigorous training program

00:14:43.265 --> 00:14:46.580
for our agents to conduct
protection operations.

00:14:46.580 --> 00:14:49.203
I would also add that there's
only so much you can do

00:14:49.203 --> 00:14:51.450
in training for observation skills.

00:14:51.450 --> 00:14:54.550
Some people have innate,
really well, inherent

00:14:54.550 --> 00:14:57.630
observation skills, but it
is also a learned skill.

00:14:57.630 --> 00:14:59.250
It's something you have to put time into

00:14:59.250 --> 00:15:01.660
and discipline, and you can learn it.

00:15:01.660 --> 00:15:05.330
I would probably give that
first scene maybe a four.

00:15:05.330 --> 00:15:06.730
But the second scene, I like.

00:15:06.730 --> 00:15:08.350
I think it's pretty realistic, actually.

00:15:08.350 --> 00:15:10.417
So I'm gonna give that one an eight.

00:15:10.417 --> 00:15:11.675
Budd: Go, go!

00:15:11.675 --> 00:15:12.508
[gunshot blasts]

00:15:13.490 --> 00:15:15.263
Stay down! The bullets
can pierce the windows,

00:15:15.263 --> 00:15:17.112
but they can't get
through the armored metal.

00:15:17.112 --> 00:15:18.470
Control. Sierra Zulu seven nine,

00:15:18.470 --> 00:15:20.036
status zero, Thornton Circus.

00:15:20.036 --> 00:15:21.350
Ken: He's maintaining calm.

00:15:21.350 --> 00:15:23.500
He's maintaining radio communications

00:15:23.500 --> 00:15:26.320
to make sure he gets help
there as quickly as possible.

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:27.890
That's the first thing to do.

00:15:27.890 --> 00:15:29.440
And he's trying to keep her calm

00:15:29.440 --> 00:15:30.788
and guide her in what to do.

00:15:30.788 --> 00:15:32.210
They have a high-powered
rifle that went through

00:15:32.210 --> 00:15:34.090
the ballistic glass but
won't go through the armor.

00:15:34.090 --> 00:15:36.110
So he has to stay down,
she has to stay down

00:15:36.110 --> 00:15:37.603
to try and maintain safety.

00:15:41.687 --> 00:15:43.023
[gunshot blasts]

00:15:44.023 --> 00:15:46.100
Would I exit that armored vehicle

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:48.900
not knowing exactly where
the threat is out there

00:15:48.900 --> 00:15:51.090
to accommodate that
and maybe get a picture

00:15:51.090 --> 00:15:53.880
with my phone to try and find them? No.

00:15:53.880 --> 00:15:55.530
That part I think is unrealistic.

00:15:56.508 --> 00:15:58.291
[Budd groaning]

00:16:00.433 --> 00:16:01.533
[car engine starts]

00:16:03.090 --> 00:16:05.010
That other agent, they make it very clear,

00:16:05.010 --> 00:16:07.930
it's what we call a
DOA, or dead on arrival.

00:16:07.930 --> 00:16:10.560
That person is not someone
who needs medical attention.

00:16:10.560 --> 00:16:12.560
You might want to climb
right on top of them

00:16:12.560 --> 00:16:13.790
to get that vehicle out of there.

00:16:13.790 --> 00:16:16.128
This is a dire situation.

00:16:17.788 --> 00:16:20.530
[tires screeching]

00:16:20.530 --> 00:16:22.357
Performance driving is being able to do

00:16:22.357 --> 00:16:23.260
one of those J-turns,

00:16:23.260 --> 00:16:25.980
which is a fancy way for
turning around very quickly

00:16:25.980 --> 00:16:28.348
and just being able to drive

00:16:28.348 --> 00:16:30.081
at a high rate of speed in reverse.

00:16:30.081 --> 00:16:31.198
It's not that easy.

00:16:31.198 --> 00:16:32.760
So these are things
that you would train in.

00:16:32.760 --> 00:16:34.100
That's an armored vehicle.

00:16:34.100 --> 00:16:36.880
They have special training,
which I've been to.

00:16:36.880 --> 00:16:39.100
And my agents, many of them have been to.

00:16:39.100 --> 00:16:40.310
To drive armored vehicles,

00:16:40.310 --> 00:16:42.710
they're much heavier
than a regular vehicle.

00:16:42.710 --> 00:16:44.560
They handle much differently.

00:16:44.560 --> 00:16:47.563
They stop slower. They accelerate slower.

00:16:50.710 --> 00:16:52.930
Budd: Take care of the P.
Officer: Sarge.

00:16:52.930 --> 00:16:55.420
Ken: So, he gets off the X. I like that.

00:16:55.420 --> 00:16:58.140
When he stops, he has his
follow vehicle with him.

00:16:58.140 --> 00:16:59.080
I like that.

00:16:59.080 --> 00:17:00.830
That's where the realism stops.

00:17:00.830 --> 00:17:03.880
The problem with law-enforcement officers,

00:17:03.880 --> 00:17:05.898
if there is one, using this type of work,

00:17:05.898 --> 00:17:08.290
is that it's very different
law-enforcement work.

00:17:08.290 --> 00:17:10.030
Someone has a gun, there's a bad guy,

00:17:10.030 --> 00:17:12.550
somebody who's shooting
people, you go towards them.

00:17:12.550 --> 00:17:15.270
But in protection work,
that's not your problem.

00:17:15.270 --> 00:17:16.623
I'd give it an eight.

00:17:17.862 --> 00:17:20.837
[crowd cheering]

00:17:20.837 --> 00:17:21.670
[gunshot blasts]

00:17:22.730 --> 00:17:24.110
That's part of our job,

00:17:24.110 --> 00:17:27.400
to take a shot for a client, a principal.

00:17:27.400 --> 00:17:28.541
What I don't like is that

00:17:28.541 --> 00:17:30.610
he pulls his weapon immediately.

00:17:30.610 --> 00:17:33.690
He spots this person that
he identifies as a threat.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:34.910
Nobody else has.

00:17:34.910 --> 00:17:37.680
He would probably be the
person tackled if he did that.

00:17:37.680 --> 00:17:39.150
I could tell you, in these awards,

00:17:39.150 --> 00:17:41.460
and I've done security at many of them,

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:43.140
there's no weapons allowed.

00:17:43.140 --> 00:17:45.290
Even off-duty police officers

00:17:45.290 --> 00:17:48.031
who are now hired to
do protection services,

00:17:48.031 --> 00:17:49.200
at these venues,

00:17:49.200 --> 00:17:50.450
they're not even allowed
to bring weapons in.

00:17:50.450 --> 00:17:51.653
Nobody gets weapons in.

00:17:51.653 --> 00:17:53.790
That's the idea. It's a
weapons-free environment.

00:17:53.790 --> 00:17:56.065
What we do is

00:17:56.065 --> 00:17:58.215
heighten sense of observation, obviously,

00:17:58.215 --> 00:17:59.500
because it's a big crowd.

00:17:59.500 --> 00:18:01.800
The good news is

00:18:01.800 --> 00:18:04.116
at those large award shows like that,

00:18:04.116 --> 00:18:05.850
not just anybody could get in there.

00:18:05.850 --> 00:18:07.340
They're vetted.

00:18:07.340 --> 00:18:10.140
People are on a guest's
list with credentials.

00:18:10.140 --> 00:18:12.870
I've worked those lines
checking those credentials

00:18:12.870 --> 00:18:14.120
for people to get in.

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:16.980
With that being said, just
like in this scenario,

00:18:16.980 --> 00:18:20.392
can somebody get press
credentials? Perhaps.

00:18:20.392 --> 00:18:22.750
I mean, it doesn't take
much to get them nowadays.

00:18:22.750 --> 00:18:25.448
And I've seen that, where
someone with press credentials

00:18:25.448 --> 00:18:28.000
has gotten into a green room at an event

00:18:28.000 --> 00:18:29.060
they shouldn't have been at.

00:18:29.060 --> 00:18:30.460
They weren't a legitimate journalist.

00:18:30.460 --> 00:18:32.800
Everybody probably has their own security.

00:18:32.800 --> 00:18:33.890
They don't get to bring them in

00:18:33.890 --> 00:18:35.150
and sit at their table with them.

00:18:35.150 --> 00:18:38.327
Those people at most would be backstage,

00:18:38.327 --> 00:18:40.810
waiting and observing. It's a rotation.

00:18:40.810 --> 00:18:42.470
As the celebrities come out,

00:18:42.470 --> 00:18:43.786
their security person waits

00:18:43.786 --> 00:18:45.261
and then escorts them back out

00:18:45.261 --> 00:18:48.010
to their green room or
wherever they're going to exit.

00:18:48.010 --> 00:18:49.743
Also sometimes I've been at events

00:18:49.743 --> 00:18:51.434
when they hired one company

00:18:51.434 --> 00:18:53.390
to do all the security for these people.

00:18:53.390 --> 00:18:56.039
So you would have,
though, someone assigned

00:18:56.039 --> 00:18:58.457
to specifically escorting
them back and forth,

00:18:58.457 --> 00:19:00.232
all of these people, to
wherever they're going,

00:19:00.232 --> 00:19:04.720
till they get to their
own exit or security team.

00:19:04.720 --> 00:19:07.943
My firm was hired at one point to do

00:19:07.943 --> 00:19:09.768
large-scale political event

00:19:09.768 --> 00:19:12.260
with multiple presidential candidates.

00:19:12.260 --> 00:19:16.130
And I had to provide probably 40 agents,

00:19:16.130 --> 00:19:18.593
because we would have someone assigned

00:19:18.593 --> 00:19:20.618
to each one of those candidates

00:19:20.618 --> 00:19:22.451
the entire time they were there.

00:19:22.451 --> 00:19:23.453
I'll give it a six.

00:19:24.800 --> 00:19:27.770
Moira: I want to introduce
you to someone, John Diggle.

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:29.510
He'll be accompanying you from now on.

00:19:29.510 --> 00:19:30.960
Oliver: I don't need a babysitter.

00:19:30.960 --> 00:19:34.920
Ken: Having clients that oppose
having protection services

00:19:34.920 --> 00:19:36.045
or they have it against their will

00:19:36.045 --> 00:19:38.154
is something that definitely happens.

00:19:38.154 --> 00:19:40.040
John: I don't want there to
be any confusion, Mr. Queen.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:41.548
My ability to keep you from harm

00:19:41.548 --> 00:19:43.400
will outweigh your comfort.

00:19:43.400 --> 00:19:44.550
Do we have an agreement?

00:19:44.550 --> 00:19:46.300
Ken: You don't talk to
a principal that way

00:19:46.300 --> 00:19:48.340
that he does, where
you call all the shots.

00:19:48.340 --> 00:19:50.810
I like to point this out, where ultimately

00:19:50.810 --> 00:19:53.070
the client really does
make the final decisions,

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:55.150
even with the president
of the United States.

00:19:55.150 --> 00:19:56.887
Oliver: Got your eyes open?

00:19:56.887 --> 00:19:57.870
John: That's what I'm here for, sir.

00:19:57.870 --> 00:19:59.930
That and answering patronizing questions.

00:19:59.930 --> 00:20:01.530
Ken: They don't want you
following them around

00:20:01.530 --> 00:20:03.845
with an earpiece on and making it

00:20:03.845 --> 00:20:06.230
very high profile that they have security.

00:20:06.230 --> 00:20:07.510
You might sit in the corner of the room

00:20:07.510 --> 00:20:08.783
and just observe things.

00:20:10.469 --> 00:20:12.175
[gunshot blasts]
[crowd yelling]

00:20:12.175 --> 00:20:13.058
John: I have to get you out of here.

00:20:13.058 --> 00:20:13.999
Oliver: No, them.

00:20:13.999 --> 00:20:15.343
Them.

00:20:15.343 --> 00:20:16.810
Ken: You're always gonna watch the people

00:20:16.810 --> 00:20:19.010
in close proximity to your principal.

00:20:19.010 --> 00:20:23.370
You're gonna see if anyone
has an unnatural focus

00:20:23.370 --> 00:20:26.440
or level of attention on your principal.

00:20:26.440 --> 00:20:27.480
You're gonna watch for things,

00:20:27.480 --> 00:20:29.110
anything that might be suspicious

00:20:29.110 --> 00:20:32.900
or seem like somebody could
be unstable or dangerous.

00:20:32.900 --> 00:20:34.510
And anytime they interact with someone,

00:20:34.510 --> 00:20:36.290
you're gonna just keep an eye on that.

00:20:36.290 --> 00:20:38.660
You're not gonna hover over
there around their back

00:20:38.660 --> 00:20:40.720
and ready to attack anyone at any time.

00:20:40.720 --> 00:20:41.763
It's unrealistic.

00:20:41.763 --> 00:20:43.430
This is not a high realism rating,

00:20:43.430 --> 00:20:44.880
no, for multiple reasons.

00:20:44.880 --> 00:20:47.258
I would give it a two.

